WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.540
It's a miracle any movies ever get made, given
everything that can go wrong.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.710
With hundreds of people using dangerous equipment
while spending tens of millions of dollars,

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.019
there are so many variables that, sometimes,
filmmakers have to call the whole thing off

00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:15.130
— all because someone did something dumb.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:26.700
Here are some movies that got canceled because
of an idiot mistake.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.439
Star Trek: Planet of the Titans

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:34.690
Star Trek was canceled after three seasons
in 1969, but its popularity exploded in syndicated

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:35.690
reruns.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:40.399
By 1977, the series was set to hit the big
screen with a film version called Star Trek:

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:43.800
Planet of the Titans, where the Enterprise
and Captain Kirk would face off against the

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.600
giants of Greek mythology.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.710
But just before filming was set to begin,
Paramount president Barry Diller canceled

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:54.100
the whole thing for a really stupid reason:
Star Wars was already scheduled to arrive

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.059
in theaters that year and he didn't think
fans could like sci-fi enough to support two

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:00.100
space epics in the same year.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:05.240
Uh, way to totally completely misread the
entire industry, Barry.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.849
"Kahn"

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.520
Justice League: Mortal

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.729
Long before Marvel crushed the box office
with Avengers, Warner Bros. was planning its

00:01:12.729 --> 00:01:17.010
own superhero team-up in the form of Justice
League: Mortal, which went into production

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.320
in 2007.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.119
With Mad Max: Fury Road director George Miller
set to direct, the film had a budget of $200

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:27.850
million and had already begun rehearsing with
a cast that included Armie Hammer as Batman,

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.619
Megan Gale as Wonder Woman, and the rapper
Common as Green Lantern.

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:34.540
Just one problem: they forgot to actually
finish writing the script.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.350
And before they could get around to it, the
Writers Guild of America went on strike.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:42.329
By the time the strike ended, the actors had
moved on to other projects and the whole film

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:44.310
had to be scrapped.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.680
Whoops!

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.049
Newt

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:53.700
In 2008, Pixar announced its slate of films
for the coming years, and among them was a

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.759
movie called Newt.

00:01:54.759 --> 00:01:59.039
The plot: "What happens when the last remaining
male and female blue-footed newts on the planet

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:03.080
are forced together by science to save the
species, and they can't stand each other?"

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.340
It was so far along in production that Pixar
animators left little Easter eggs referencing

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.760
Newt in Toy Story 3, such as the "Newt Crossing"
sign on Andy's bedroom door.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.239
But in 2011, Fox Animation Studios released
Rio, which had almost the exact same plot,

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:20.910
only with birds instead of lizards — and
which had reportedly been in production since

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.510
around 2007, one year before Newt was announced.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:28.269
Pixar panicked, worried that Newt would be
considered a rip-off, and canceled it entirely.

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:31.439
Strange, they didn't do that with Antz and
A Bug's Life…

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.520
"Ok, can we stop.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.380
Get me Cindy on the phone."

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.390
Inhumans

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:42.709
It sure looked like Inhumans — which features
Marvel characters who are kinda like mutants,

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:46.920
but also kinda from space — would be the
next massive on-screen team-up for Marvel.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.709
Announced for a 2018 release, the film seemed
to be built up from two entire seasons' worth

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:54.569
of storylines on ABC's Marvel's Agents of
S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:54.569 --> 00:02:57.299
But then Inhumans was quietly dropped from
the schedule.

00:02:57.299 --> 00:02:58.299
Why?

00:02:58.299 --> 00:02:59.299
There are a couple theories.

00:02:59.299 --> 00:03:03.040
Because of the disconnect between Marvel's
TV efforts and its movie business, Marvel

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.030
Studios may not have been happy at having
the Inhumans movie's details dictated by a

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.140
television sho w with middling ratings.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:14.460
Meanwhile, rumor has it that Inhumans was
the pet project of Marvel CEO Ike Perlmutter.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.709
And when Perlmutter was ousted from his role
at Marvel Studios in favor of studio president

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:23.040
Kevin Feige, Feige dumped the film to stick
it to his rival and pursue his own vision

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:24.631
instead, namely Captain Marvel.

00:03:24.631 --> 00:03:26.211
"Captain Marvel herself.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.240
Brie Larson."

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.860
Although, when you think about it, the biggest
mistake of all might've been Marvel selling

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.300
the X-Men movie rights to Fox way back in
the 1990s.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.110
If they hadn't done that, we'd never have
had to hear about the Inhumans movie in the

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:44.540
first place...

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.269
A Revenge of the Nerds remake

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:51.849
In 2006, Fox Atomic began filming a modern-day
reboot of the classic '80s college movie series

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:55.370
Revenge of the Nerds at Emory University in
Atlanta.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.810
And then school officials actually read the
script.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:03.019
Here's actual footage from when Emory University's
president finished looking at the screenplay:

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:04.019
"Ahhhhh"

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:08.019
Anyway, after that, the college immediately
pulled out of the project, even though much

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:09.989
of the movie had already been filmed.

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:13.379
Producers were forced to complete the movie
at tiny Agnes Scott College.

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:17.660
When Fox Atomic honcho Peter Rice saw how
rinky dink the movie looked as a result, he

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.700
decided to just scrap the entire thing.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.780
Who made the bigger mistake: Emory for agreeing
to participate in the movie before reading

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:24.780
the script?

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.760
Or Fox for actually caring about production
values on Revenge of the Nerds?

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:29.860
"You know Karate."

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:30.440
"No"

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:31.440
"Good"

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.540
Two sequels to Terminator: Salvation

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:40.321
In 2009, Terminator: Salvation earned $371
million worldwide, thanks in part to the star

00:04:40.321 --> 00:04:44.940
power of Christian Bale as the time-traveling,
robot-killing, humanity-saving John Connor.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.820
Two sequels were planned — except the Halcyon
Company, which produced the movie, went bankrupt.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:53.540
Even with all that revenue, through some kind
of financial idiocy, they couldn't pay off

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.820
the $39 million they owed the hedge fund Pacificor.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:00.350
In order to pay their debt, Halcyon auctioned
off the rights to Terminator.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.150
The winning bid? $29.5 million, from…

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:03.960
Pacificor.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.040
Nice exit strategy!

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.260
"Come with me if you want to live."

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:09.270
Dumb and Dumber 2

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:15.410
Here's a real what if: way back in 1998, South
Park creators Trey Parker and Matt Stone signed

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:21.020
a $1.5 million deal with New Line Cinema to
write a prequel to the 1994 hit comedy Dumb

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.020
and Dumber.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.660
It sounds like a match made in heaven.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.580
"So you're telling me there's a chance."

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.410
So what was the idiot mistake?

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:32.050
Turns out Parker and Stone didn't actually
want to do the project in the first place.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:36.340
They eventually gave the money back, leading
New Line to hire other writers for what eventually

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:41.750
turned out to be the universally panned 2003
prequel Dumb and Dumberer: When Harry Met

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:42.750
Lloyd.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:43.750
Hey, I like that movie.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:44.750
The Adventures of Fartman

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:50.360
Howard Stern first created his superhero alter-ego
Fartman back in 1981, and turned it into a

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:55.990
pre-internet meme with a massively popular
appearance at the 1992 MTV Video Music Awards.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:57.590
"How'd that ass feel Luke?"

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.280
"Great Ass, great ass, man"

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:01.280
"Yeah"

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.530
It was such a hit that New Line Cinema started
production on a feature film called The Adventures

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:09.910
of Fartman, which screenwriter J.F. Lawton
promised would be just as crude and lewd as

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:11.920
you'd expect from a Howard Stern movie.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.650
However, when executives got wind of what
Lawton was planning, they asked him to tone

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.880
it down and make it a mainstream PG-13 film
instead.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.200
Stern immediately quit in protest at the dumb
request, and that was the end of Fartman.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:25.220
"Fartman"

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.200
Oh, well.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.580
The Queen/Freddie Mercury biopic

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:35.150
Comedian Sacha Baron Cohen was once set to
play the legendary lead singer of Queen, Freddie

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.440
Mercury, who died in 1991.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.530
It seemed like the perfect fit, since Cohen
is not only a big fan, he also bears more

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:43.560
than a passing resemblance to Mercury.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:47.190
But Cohen backed out of the project when he
discovered that the surviving members of Queen

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:51.160
wanted the second half of the film to focus
not on Mercury, but on how great the band

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.010
has continued to be since his death.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:54.940
Two more Fletch sequels

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.800
Eight years after Chevy Chase last played
smug and smarmy reporter I.M. Fletch in 1989's

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:05.050
Fletch Lives, the franchise was set to make
a comeback thanks to filmmaker Kevin Smith.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:09.160
Smith wanted the film to focus on Fletch's
daughter, to be played by his then-girlfriend

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:10.720
Joey Lauren Adams.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.120
But after Smith and Chase squabbled over a
plan to have Chase reprise his role as Fletch,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.770
the entire project was scrapped.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.760
Apparently Chase had something better to do.

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.920
"Yeah, I was just passing through town and
ah, looking for something to do this evening.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.750
There was nothing going on at the rotary club
and I heard about this."

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:28.800
Not to be deterred, Smith later convinced
Miramax to buy the rights to the franchise,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.710
this time for an origin prequel that would
star his buddy Jason Lee and wouldn't involve

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:33.710
Chase at all.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:37.560
But Miramax didn't think Lee was a big enough
star and shut the whole thing down.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.130
Looks like Chase had the last laugh.

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.740
Thanks for watching!

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:47.120
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:50.830
And leave us a comment to let us know which
of these movies you most wish had been made…

